CertHub.register({"id":"server-plus","vendor":"CompTIA","name":"CompTIA Server+","short":"Server+","exam":"SK0-005","blurb":"Vendor-neutral server certification for administrators: hardware, storage, operating systems, virtualization, security, backup and troubleshooting in on-premises and hybrid environments.","status":"check","statusNote":"Could not read the official page during this task: comptia.org and partners.comptia.org were blocked by the network proxy on 2026-09-24. Exam code, domains, weights (18/30/24/28), length, time and passing score come from the published SK0-005 objectives as reported by search results and training providers. Web searches found no announced SK0-006 or SK0-005 retirement date; confirm both on comptia.org before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"Up to 90 (multiple choice and performance-based)","minutes":90,"pass":"750 on a 100–900 scale"},"examSim":{"questions":90,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"CompTIA Server+ certification page","url":"https://www.comptia.org/en-us/certifications/server/"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Server hardware installation and management","w":18,"topics":["Rack planning: rack units, rail kits, weight distribution (heaviest at the bottom), airflow, hot and cold aisles, cable management arms","Server form factors: tower, rack mount, blade enclosures","Power: voltage, redundant power supplies, PDUs, UPS sizing and runtime, generators, separate circuits, power connector types","Network cabling and connectors: Cat5e/Cat6/Cat6a, single-mode vs multimode fiber, SFP/SFP+/QSFP transceivers, twinax/DAC, labeling","Drive types: HDD speeds (7.2K/10K/15K), SSD, NVMe, SAS vs SATA, hot-swap and hot-plug","RAID levels 0, 1, 5, 6, 10: fault tolerance, usable capacity, write penalty; hardware vs software RAID; JBOD","Storage architectures: DAS, NAS, SAN, iSCSI, Fibre Channel, FCoE; capacity planning and base-2 vs base-10 sizing","Out-of-band management: iLO, iDRAC, IPMI/BMC, remote KVM, IP KVM, crash cart","Firmware, BIOS and UEFI settings, Secure Boot, TPM, boot order, driver and firmware update planning","Hardware components: CPUs and cores, memory types (ECC, registered), expansion cards, fans and hot-swappable parts"],"notes":["Objectives 1.1–1.3"],"labs":["Use a free rack planner (draw.io's rack shapes work) to lay out a 42U rack with two UPSs, a switch, a SAN and six 1U/2U servers, marking hot/cold aisle airflow and power feeds A and B.","In VirtualBox, attach four virtual disks to an Ubuntu Server VM, build a RAID 5 array with `mdadm`, fail one disk (`mdadm --fail`) and rebuild it while watching `/proc/mdstat`.","Calculate usable capacity for six 4 TB drives in RAID 1, 5, 6 and 10, then open your own PC's UEFI setup (or a VM's firmware menu) and find Secure Boot, TPM and boot order settings."]},{"id":2,"name":"Server administration","w":30,"topics":["OS installation: minimum requirements, HCL, bare metal vs virtual, GUI vs core/headless installs, partitioning and file systems (NTFS, ReFS, ext4, XFS, VMFS)","Installation methods: media, PXE/network boot, imaging and cloning, templates, answer files, P2V","Network services: static vs DHCP addressing, DNS, NTP, NIC teaming/bonding, VLAN tagging, firewall ports, IPv4 and IPv6","Server roles: web, application, database, file and print, directory services, DNS/DHCP, mail, messaging, NTP, and how roles relate to hardware sizing","High availability: clustering (active-active vs active-passive), heartbeat, quorum, load balancing methods (round robin, least connections), failover and failback","Redundancy: NIC teaming, multipathing (MPIO), redundant power and storage, fault tolerance vs high availability","Virtualization: Type 1 vs Type 2 hypervisors, host vs guest, resource allocation and overcommitment, virtual switches and NICs, snapshots, templates, VM migration","Cloud models: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS; public, private, hybrid; on-premises vs cloud-hosted servers","Scripting basics: Bash, PowerShell, batch, Python; variables, loops, conditionals, comparators; common uses (user setup, log checks, scheduled tasks)","Asset management and documentation: labeling, inventory, warranty, life-cycle, baselines, diagrams, change management, SLAs, secure storage of documents","Licensing models: per socket, per core, per user, per device/CAL, site, subscription, open source; license compliance and version compatibility"],"notes":["Objectives 2.1–2.8"],"labs":["Install Proxmox VE or Hyper-V (Windows Pro) as a free hypervisor, create a Windows Server evaluation VM and an Ubuntu Server VM, then take a snapshot, change something and revert.","On the Windows Server evaluation VM, add the DNS and DHCP roles, create a scope with a reservation, and set a static IP with PowerShell (`New-NetIPAddress`, `Set-DnsClientServerAddress`).","Write a PowerShell or Bash script that loops through a CSV of usernames and creates each account, then schedule a second script that reports free disk space daily (Task Scheduler or cron)."]},{"id":3,"name":"Security and disaster recovery","w":24,"topics":["Data security: encryption at rest and in transit, data retention, data storage location, UEFI/BIOS passwords, bootloader password","Physical security: locked racks and cages, mantraps/access vestibules, badge readers, biometrics, cameras, security guards, fire suppression","Identity and access management: least privilege, role-based access, groups, MFA, SSO, account lockout, password policies, service accounts, auditing","Data security risks: data loss, unencrypted media, insider threats, malware and ransomware; mitigation with DLP, patching and segmentation","Server hardening: disable unused services and ports, remove unneeded software, OS and firmware patching, host firewall, antivirus/EDR, secure admin protocols","Decommissioning and media destruction: wiping, degaussing, shredding, crushing, certificates of destruction, asset records","Backup types: full, incremental, differential, synthetic full, snapshot; backup media and rotation (grandfather-father-son), 3-2-1 rule","Backup operations: frequency, retention, on-site vs off-site storage, integrity checks, test restores","Disaster recovery: hot, warm and cold sites, cloud DR, replication (synchronous vs asynchronous), RPO and RTO, DR plan testing (tabletop, live failover)","Business continuity: BIA, MTBF and MTTR, prioritizing critical services, communication plans"],"notes":["Objectives 3.1–3.8"],"labs":["Harden an Ubuntu Server VM: disable password SSH login in favour of keys, turn on `ufw` allowing only needed ports, remove an unused package and run `ss -tulpn` before and after to compare.","Set up Veeam Agent Free (Windows) or `restic`/`rsync` (Linux) to back up a folder to a second virtual disk, run a full then an incremental backup, then delete a file and restore it.","Write a one-page DR plan for a small office file server with an RPO and RTO, a backup rotation and a test schedule, then walk through it as a tabletop exercise."]},{"id":4,"name":"Troubleshooting","w":28,"topics":["The CompTIA troubleshooting methodology: identify, theory, test, plan, implement, verify, document","Hardware problems: POST errors and beep codes, overheating, failed fans and power supplies, memory errors, predictive failure alerts, LED indicators","Storage problems: degraded or failed RAID arrays, controller battery/cache issues, disk full, slow I/O, mount failures, boot device not found, corrupted file systems","Storage tools: disk management, fsck/chkdsk, RAID controller utilities, SMART data, partitioning tools","OS and software problems: failed updates, services not starting, memory leaks, runaway processes, driver issues, boot loops, misconfigured applications","OS tools: Event Viewer, system logs and journalctl, Task Manager/top, Performance Monitor, rollback of updates, safe mode","Network problems: no connectivity, wrong IP/mask/gateway, DNS resolution failures, duplex mismatch, firewall rules, NIC teaming misconfiguration","Network tools: ping, tracert/traceroute, nslookup/dig, ipconfig/ip, netstat/ss, arp, telnet or Test-NetConnection for port tests","Security problems: permissions and access denied errors, expired certificates, antivirus quarantining files, firewall blocking services, compromised accounts","Using logs, baselines and performance counters to find root cause"],"notes":["Objectives 4.1–4.6"],"labs":["Break your Windows Server evaluation VM on purpose (stop a service, set a wrong DNS server, fill the disk), then use Event Viewer, `Get-Service`, `Test-NetConnection` and `Get-PSDrive` to find and fix each fault.","On an Ubuntu Server VM, create a runaway process (`yes > /dev/null &`), find it with `top` and `ps`, kill it, then read the related entries with `journalctl -p err -b`.","Install smartmontools, run `smartctl -a` on a disk, and write down which attributes (reallocated sectors, pending sectors) would make you replace the drive before it fails."]}],"study":{"1":[["Why do you install the heaviest equipment at the bottom of a rack?","It keeps the center of gravity low so the rack does not tip when servers slide out on rails, and it makes lifting and installation safer. UPS units and large storage arrays usually go at the bottom."],["Compare RAID 5, RAID 6 and RAID 10 for fault tolerance and usable space with eight 2 TB drives.","RAID 5 survives one failed drive and gives 14 TB. RAID 6 survives two failed drives and gives 12 TB. RAID 10 survives one drive per mirror pair and gives 8 TB, with the best write performance and fastest rebuilds."],["What is the difference between DAS, NAS and SAN?","DAS is storage attached directly to one server. NAS is a file-level share served over the network (SMB/NFS). SAN is a dedicated network that presents block-level storage (LUNs) over Fibre Channel or iSCSI."],["What can you do with a BMC such as iLO or iDRAC that you cannot do over the normal network?","Power the server on, off or cycle it, watch POST and the console with remote KVM, mount virtual media to install an OS, read hardware health and logs, and change firmware settings, even when the OS is down."],["Why might a new 4 TB drive show about 3.64 TB in the OS?","Drive vendors use base-10 (1 TB = 10^12 bytes), while many OSs report base-2 (TiB = 2^40 bytes). 4 × 10^12 / 2^40 ≈ 3.64."],["Why use two power supplies connected to separate PDUs and circuits?","If one PSU, PDU, UPS or circuit fails, the other keeps the server running, removing a single point of failure in the power path."]],"2":[["Contrast Type 1 and Type 2 hypervisors with examples.","Type 1 runs directly on hardware (ESXi, Hyper-V, Proxmox/KVM, Xen) and is used for production servers. Type 2 runs as an app on a desktop OS (VirtualBox, VMware Workstation) and suits labs and testing."],["Explain active-active vs active-passive clustering.","Active-active: all nodes serve traffic at once, sharing load, so capacity drops when one fails. Active-passive: a standby node takes over only when the active node fails, so capacity is steady but hardware sits idle."],["What is a cluster quorum and why does it matter?","Quorum is the majority vote of nodes (and often a witness) needed for the cluster to run. It prevents split-brain, where two halves of a partitioned cluster both think they own the resources."],["Why should a snapshot not be treated as a backup?","A snapshot depends on the parent disk on the same storage, so losing that storage loses both. Long-lived snapshots also grow and slow performance. Backups are independent copies kept elsewhere."],["Name three licensing models and when each costs the most.","Per core: costly on many-core CPUs. Per socket: costly with many sockets. Per user/device CAL: costly with many users or devices. Subscription models add ongoing cost but include updates."],["What should server documentation include?","Asset tags and serial numbers, warranty and support dates, rack location, IP and network diagrams, baselines, configuration and change records, licensing, and service dependencies, stored securely with a copy offline."]],"3":[["Explain full, incremental and differential backups and how a restore works for each.","Full copies everything. Incremental copies changes since the last backup of any type, so a restore needs the full plus every incremental. Differential copies changes since the last full, so a restore needs the full plus the latest differential."],["What is the 3-2-1 backup rule?","Keep three copies of data on two different types of media with one copy off-site (and ideally one offline or immutable to resist ransomware)."],["Compare hot, warm and cold sites.","Hot: fully equipped with current data, failover in minutes to hours, most expensive. Warm: hardware in place but data must be restored, hours to days. Cold: space and power only, days to weeks, cheapest."],["Which media destruction method fits each case: SSD, magnetic tape, HDD being reused?","SSD: shred or crush (degaussing does not work on flash). Tape: degauss or shred. HDD being reused inside the company: secure overwrite/wipe."],["List six server hardening steps.","Remove unneeded roles and software, disable unused services and ports, patch OS and firmware, enable a host firewall, use key-based or MFA admin access over secure protocols, rename or disable default accounts, and install endpoint protection."],["Explain RPO and RTO for a database server.","RPO is the maximum data loss allowed in time (a 15-minute RPO needs log shipping or replication every 15 minutes). RTO is how long it may take to restore service (a 2-hour RTO needs a warm standby or fast restore process)."]],"4":[["List the troubleshooting methodology steps in order.","Identify the problem (gather info, question users, check recent changes, back up if needed), establish a theory, test the theory, establish a plan and identify effects, implement the solution or escalate, verify full functionality and implement preventive measures, document findings."],["A server shows an amber LED on one drive and the array is degraded. What do you do?","Confirm with the RAID controller utility which drive failed, check that a backup is current, replace it with an identical or compatible hot-swap drive, watch the rebuild complete and document the change. Do not pull the wrong drive."],["A service will not start after a reboot. Where do you look?","Event Viewer or `journalctl -u <service>` for errors, service dependencies, the service account's password or permissions, recent updates or config changes, and whether another process is using its port (`netstat`/`ss`)."],["A server can ping other servers by IP but not by name. What do you check?","DNS client settings, DNS server reachability on port 53, the hosts file, `nslookup`/`dig` against the configured server, missing or wrong records, and the DNS suffix/search list."],["Which symptoms suggest a memory leak, and how do you confirm it?","Available memory falls steadily over days until the server slows or crashes, often fixed briefly by a restart. Confirm by trending a process's memory use in Performance Monitor or `top`/`ps` over time."],["Users get 'access denied' on a share after it was moved. What causes this?","Moving or copying can change NTFS permissions or inheritance, and share and NTFS permissions combine with the most restrictive winning. Check both, plus group membership and whether users need to log off to refresh their token."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true});
